# REGRESSION FIXTURE — the cheapest differential in this family: TWO SPELLINGS
# OF ONE INTENT that behaved OPPOSITELY, both `gg check`-clean, in one program.
#
#   inc(&v[0])                  -> was WRONG for the six by-value types
#   inc(&v.get(0).unwrap())     -> was CORRECT for all ten
#
# The get-chain spelling was right by a different mechanism: `.get()` returns a
# `Ptr(T)`, so the read already yielded a pointer and the `is_already_ptr`
# fall-through forwarded it. The index spelling read a value COPY. Nothing in the
# language distinguishes the two — same collection, same element, same sigil —
# so the split was pure implementation accident.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): the SPELLING axis of one intent, at the `Vector[int]`
# element cell — the value-typed element, which is the side that was broken. Both
# halves are asserted in the same program so a future change that fixes one and
# breaks the other cannot pass.
#
# RED-VERIFIED, and note the two halves need DIFFERENT forms of red (Core #12's
# ⚠ clause):
#   * The `&v[0]` half: RED against the pre-fix compiler, which printed `10`.
#   * ⚠ The `&v.get(0).unwrap()` half is NOT RED-VERIFIED, and this header
#     previously claimed it was. The claim was: "delete the `is_already_ptr`
#     fall-through guard and this half emits `**T` and the assertion fails."
#     THAT DOES NOT REPRODUCE — measured by disabling that exact guard and
#     re-running this family: everything stays GREEN, including this row. The
#     reason is one layer down: `lir/lower/operands.rs` emits `SlotLoad` (reading
#     the pointer bits) rather than `SlotAddr` for a bare place whose local is
#     `SlotKind::BorrowedPtr`-kinded, and a `.get().unwrap()` result IS such a
#     local — so the LIR already forwards the pointer and the GIR-level guard is
#     not what saves this row.
#
#     WHAT THIS HALF ACTUALLY PINS, then: that the two spellings AGREE. It is a
#     DIFFERENTIAL, not an independent guard — its value is that a future change
#     which fixes or breaks one spelling without the other fails here. It was
#     green before this round and is green after; that is the claim, and it is
#     worth pinning because the two spellings measurably DISAGREED for five
#     months. (A shape that genuinely reds the `is_already_ptr` guard does exist
#     — `MutRef[Vector[int]]`, a `PtrTo` slot whose pointee is not
#     `GorgetString`, so the LIR takes `SlotAddr` — and it is pinned as row 4 of
#     `cow_amp_ref_field_forward.gg`. It is NOT this shape.)
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: the index half pins `try_resolve_place`'s `Index` arm and
# its `Projection::Deref` normalisation (postcondition 1); the get-chain half
# pins the surviving `is_already_ptr` fall-through.
#
# Seeds are computed, never literals.
int seed():
    return 10

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void setv(int &n):
    n = 142

void main():
    Vector[int] v = Vector[int]()
    v.push(seed())

    # Spelling A — subscript. Was silently wrong.
    inc(&v[0])
    print(v[0])

    # Spelling B — get-chain. Was already correct; pins the fall-through guard.
    setv(&v.get(0).unwrap())
    print(v[0])
